<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cherven Towns</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cherven cities</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cherven Towns were a group of fortified settlements in the borderland between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, in the area of present-day south-eastern Poland and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, named after the stronghold of Cherven. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> records that Vladimir I took them from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> in 981. They changed hands several times until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> recovered them in 1031, and later formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cherven-towns.jpg" alt="Cherven Towns" width="320" height="594" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lonio17 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grody_czerwienskie.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The name comes from the gord of Cherven, the region's main town, which historians identify with the stronghold beside the village of Czermno in Tomaszów Lubelski County, in easternmost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Cherven is derived from the Slavic word for worm, after the larvae of the insect Porphyrophora polonica, from which a valued red dye was made for export to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. The name was later read as the word for red, which gave rise to the names Red Towns and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a>.</p><p>The extent of the region is not entirely clear. Written sources name only the strongholds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl">Przemyśl</a> and Cherven. The name probably first meant a territory between the Bug and Wieprz rivers, west of the Bug and east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> region, and it may have included the lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelm">Chełm</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Before the Rus' conquest the borderland lay between several powers. Cosmas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> relates that the Přemyslid rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> controlled the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> until 999, and the traveller Abraham ben Jacob wrote in 965 that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Boleslaus II of Bohemia</a> ruled from Prague to Kraków. Historians assume that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I of Poland</a> took over the region in the 970s. Whether its people belonged to a West Slavic or an East Slavic tribe, and whether they were independent of both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and Rus', is disputed between Polish and Ukrainian-Russian historiography; the historian Leontii Voitovych has speculated that they may have formed an independent tribal union with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a> to Bohemia.</p><p>The towns were taken by the Polish prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-i-the-brave">Bolesław I the Brave</a> in 1018. In the campaign of 1031 that won them back for Grand Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>, Harald, chief of Yaroslav's bodyguard, fought in his army, and between 1034 and 1039 Yaroslav most likely founded the gord of Sutiejsk to guard the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. In 1085 the region became the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl">Peremyshl</a> under the lordship of Rus'.</p><p>In 1240 the region was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and the Cherven Cities appear in written sources for the last time in 1289. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iii-the-great">Casimir III the Great</a> acquired the territory for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> in 1340. From the 15th century the term <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-ruthenia">Red Ruthenia</a>, taken from the misread name of the towns, was used for the new Ruthenian Voivodeship, which took in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelm">Chełm</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl">Przemyśl</a> regions as well as those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lviv</a> and Halych.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cherven Cities” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
